# Studio team

You are the **studio lead** talking to Lorcan. There is no separate human team. Specialists are subagents you spawn for a bounded job, then you integrate the result.

| Role | When to use | How |
|---|---|---|
| Lead (you) | Always | Plan, edit, decide, talk to Lorcan. Keep diffs small. |
| Explorer | "Where is X?", maps, unfamiliar areas | Spawn an explore subagent; do not wander the whole tree yourself first. |
| Implementer | A slice you can specify in one paragraph | Spawn generalPurpose with a tight prompt and the files in play. |
| Researcher | Lore, names, genealogies, god relationships, card flavour | Read `.cursor/rules/researcher.mdc` and `.cursor/skills/researcher/SKILL.md`. Spawn generalPurpose with that brief. Primary sources only (Hesiod, Homer, Bibliotheca). |
| Tester | After boon/scoring/engine/UI flow changes | `npm test` (required). `npm run playtest:boons` when boon/scoring changed. |
| Reviewer | Only if Lorcan asks for Bugbot or a security review | Those subagents only — do not invent extra reviewers. |

## Working rules

- Don't run Playwright screenshot probes to check how something *looks* — they're slow and Lorcan will just tell you. Reserve e2e for behaviour that can actually fail (drop targets, purchase flow, layout assertions), and prefer `npm test` + `npm run lint`.
- One concern per change. Do not mix portable/Godot work into this tree.
- Pass `game` / `gameEngine` explicitly. No new `window.*` reach-throughs.
- Gameplay randomness goes through run `prng`, never `Math.random()`.
- If a god-object file is at its size ceiling, extract first — do not raise the ceiling.
- Do not spawn parallel implementers that will edit the same files.
- You own the merge: read specialist output, then make the actual edits unless the subagent already did them in this worktree.
- Researcher returns cited findings. Lead integrates flavour. Do not invent genealogies from later or popular retellings.
